.TH "HEE\-PRINT" "1" "" "" "HEE Tools"
.SH NAME
hee\-print \- render files nicely for terminal surfaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
hee\-print [\-\-no\-header] [\-\-animate] [\-\-image\-format FMT] [\-\-] FILE...
hee\-print [\-\-no\-header] \-\-stdin [\-\-hint md|yaml|json|image|text]
SOMETHING | hee\-print [\-\-hint md|yaml|json|image|text]
hee\-print help
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IP
.EX
Picks a renderer from the file extension. When the nicer tool is missing,
it degrades to a plainer one \-\- and SAYS SO on stderr, naming what is
missing, so a plain render is never mistaken for a pretty one. Never
prompts, never writes. A degrade still exits 0: the file was rendered.

  .md/.markdown \-> glow \-p, else bat/batcat, else cat (+ WARNING)
  .yml/.yaml    \-> yq \-P (the mikefarah yq only), else bat/batcat,
                   else cat (+ WARNING naming the yq that was found)
  .json         \-> jq ., else bat/batcat, else cat (+ WARNING)
  .png/.jpg/.jpeg/.gif/.webp
                \-> chafa (a still; \-\-animate plays a GIF for 10 s), else
                   \[ga]file\[ga] plus a WARNING naming the missing renderer \-\-
                   never a raw byte dump to the terminal
  other         \-> bat/batcat \-p \-\-paging=never, else cat

On yq: Debian and Ubuntu ship a DIFFERENT \[ga]yq\[ga] \-\- a Python jq wrapper
(\[ga]yq 3.x\[ga]) that does not understand \[ga]\-P\[ga] and errors on it. hee\-print
therefore uses yq only when \[ga]yq \-\-version\[ga] reports the mikefarah yq,
and otherwise degrades with a WARNING. \[ga]hee tools\-update\[ga] installs the
pinned mikefarah yq into \[ti]/.local/bin, ahead of the distro one on PATH.

Images: the format that is known to work is the default, measured from
where the tool is running, not guessed (operator, 2026\-09\-06: \[dq]when you
find what works in tmux and not, make it the default and add a switch
to try something else\[dq]):

  inside tmux, allow\-passthrough on     \-> sixels, passed through to the
                                           outer terminal (which must draw
                                           them; tmux 3.4 cannot itself)
  inside tmux, allow\-passthrough off    \-> symbols (pixels cannot leave tmux)
  kitty (KITTY_WINDOW_ID) / WezTerm     \-> kitty
  iTerm2                                \-> iterm
  Windows Terminal (WT_SESSION)         \-> sixels
  anything else                         \-> chafa\[aq]s own guess

\-\-image\-format FMT  try something else: symbols, sixels, kitty, iterm,
                    or auto. HEE_PRINT_IMAGE_FORMAT (heerc or environment)
                    is the same override, persistent. \[ga]\-\-image\-format
                    show\[ga] prints the format that would be used and why.

\-\-no\-header  suppress the per\-file header printed when given several files
\-\-animate    let an animated image play (10 s) instead of showing a still
\-\-stdin      read the document from stdin instead of a path. Implied when
             no FILE is given and stdin is a pipe, so \[ga]foo | hee\-print\[ga]
             works \-\- answering a piped document with the usage page tells
             the reader nothing about what went wrong
\-\-hint       which renderer to use when reading stdin; ignored otherwise.
             Omit it and the bytes are sniffed with file(1), so a piped
             image renders instead of being cat\[aq]d at the terminal
.EE
